{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Run 4-Axis Plasma CAM Offline From Your Own AI Assistant</h2>
          <p>
            Keep customer STEP files and defense weldments off cloud servers, and keep cutting even when shop internet goes down.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            Hook Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, or LM Studio straight to <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code>, a local stdio <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> server on macOS, Linux, or Windows. You get 19 offline shop tools:
          </p>

          <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.85rem;">
            <li><strong>Drive math:</strong> Compute exact axis limits with <code class="mono">rack_pinion_travel</code>, <code class="mono">lead_screw_travel</code>, and <code class="mono">rotary_travel</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>STEP inspection:</strong> Run <code class="mono">analyze_step</code> on round pipe, HSS, angle, and channel (<code class="mono">STARTCUT</code>, <code class="mono">ENDCUT</code>, holes, notches).</li>
            <li><strong>Zero-bevel copes:</strong> Apply <code class="mono">apply_zero_bevel</code> for tight saddle weld fit-up.</li>
            <li><strong>G-code &amp; stick nesting:</strong> Run <code class="mono">generate_gcode</code> for 4D velocity scaling and <code class="mono">M62/M63 P1</code> torch sync.</li>
            <li><strong>3D preview:</strong> Call <code class="mono">open_in_rotarycam</code> to inspect your cut path in your browser before striking an arc.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Transport Protocol</span>
              <span class="value">Standard JSON-RPC 2.0 over Stdio</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Cloud Dependency</span>
              <span class="value">Zero (100% Offline &amp; Air-Gapped)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Supported Clients</span>
              <span class="value">Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, LM Studio</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Locate the Binary &amp; Activate Your Offline License (<code class="mono">rotarycam.lic</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              The <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code> binary sits right inside your app folder. You need no Python install, Node runtime, or package manager.
            </p>

            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Binary Locations by Platform:</strong>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong style="color: var(--amber-400);">macOS:</strong> <code class="mono">/Applications/RotaryCAM.app/Contents/MacOS/rotarycam-mcp</code> (or <code class="mono">./dist/bundle/rotarycam-mcp</code> in source builds)</li>
                  <li><strong style="color: var(--cyan-400);">Windows:</strong> <code class="mono">C:\Program Files\RotaryCAM\rotarycam-mcp.exe</code></li>
                  <li><strong style="color: var(--emerald-400);">Linux:</strong> <code class="mono">/opt/RotaryCAM/rotarycam-mcp</code></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Offline License Activation:</strong> Drop your signed <code class="mono">rotarycam.lic</code> file in the <em>same folder</em> as <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code>. The server checks your offline license key locally at startup and unlocks zero-bevel compensation, multi-stick nesting, and custom machine profiles with zero internet access.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> Terminal / Shell Verification</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code># Test that rotarycam-mcp starts and reports version
$ ./rotarycam-mcp -v
rotarycam-mcp v{{ version }} (commit de808aa, built 2026-10-23, run local)

# Verify license discovery (auto-detects rotarycam.lic in current folder)
$ ./rotarycam-mcp -license ./rotarycam.lic</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Configure Claude Desktop (<code class="mono">claude_desktop_config.json</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Point Claude Desktop to your local <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code> binary by adding this block to your config file:
            </p>

            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>macOS:</strong> <code class="mono">~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code></li>
              <li><strong>Windows:</strong> <code class="mono">%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json</code></li>
              <li><strong>Linux:</strong> <code class="mono">~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code></li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> claude_desktop_config.json</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "rotarycam": {
      "command": "/Applications/RotaryCAM.app/Contents/MacOS/rotarycam-mcp"
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Restart Claude Desktop. Check for the hammer (<span class="mono">🔨</span>) icon in the chat box to verify all 19 tools loaded.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Configure Cursor IDE &amp; VS Code</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              If you run shop automation scripts in Cursor IDE or VS Code, register your local stdio server in three clicks:
            </p>

            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>1. Open Cursor Settings:</strong> Press <code class="mono">Ctrl + Shift + J</code> (or <code class="mono">Cmd + ,</code> on macOS) and open <strong>Features → MCP Servers</strong>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>2. Click "+ Add New MCP Server":</strong>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Name:</strong> <code class="mono">rotarycam</code></li>
                  <li><strong>Type:</strong> <code class="mono">stdio</code></li>
                  <li><strong>Command:</strong> <code class="mono">/path/to/RotaryCAM/rotarycam-mcp</code> (or <code class="mono">C:\Program Files\RotaryCAM\rotarycam-mcp.exe</code> on Windows)</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>3. Refresh Connection:</strong> Click the green refresh button. A green status dot shows your editor is talking to the local CAM engine.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Run Fully Air-Gapped With Local LLMs (LM Studio / Ollama)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              For ITAR work or remote job sites with zero internet, pair <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code> with a local model (such as Qwen 2.5 Coder or Llama 3.3) in LM Studio:
            </p>

            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>1. Open LM Studio:</strong> Go to the <strong>Developer / MCP</strong> tab.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>2. Add Stdio Server:</strong> Paste the full path to your <code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code> binary.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>3. Cut Off-Grid:</strong> Your language model and CAM solver both run on your own PC. Zero CAD files or G-code ever leave your shop.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Calculate Axis Limits From Your Drive Hardware</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Stop guessing axis velocity, acceleration, and jerk limits in your controller. Ask your assistant to compute exact numbers from your gearbox, pinion, and motor specs:
            </p>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> Example: Rack-and-Pinion X-Axis Calculation</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>// User asks: "My X axis uses a 3000 RPM servo, 10:1 planetary gearbox, and a 20-tooth Module 1.5 pinion.
// What are my travel per revolution and maximum linear velocity?"

// Assistant calls rack_pinion_travel:
{
  "module": 1.5,
  "teeth": 20,
  "gearbox_ratio": 10.0
}

// Tool output:
{
  "pitch_diameter_mm": 30.0,
  "pinion_circumference_mm": 94.248,
  "travel_per_motor_rev_mm": 9.425,
  "steps_per_mm_1000cpr": 424.413
}

// Assistant calls axis_max_velocity with 3000 motor RPM and 85% safety factor:
{
  "max_motor_rpm": 3000,
  "travel_per_motor_rev": 9.425,
  "safety_factor": 0.85
}

// Tool output:
{
  "theoretical_max_velocity_mm_min": 28274.3,
  "recommended_max_velocity_mm_min": 24033.2
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              You get matching drive-math tools for ballscrews (<code class="mono">lead_screw_travel</code>), rotary chucks (<code class="mono">rotary_travel</code>), and S-curve jerk ramps (<code class="mono">axis_jerk_from_ramp</code>).
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Inspect 3D STEP Assemblies Locally (<code class="mono">analyze_step</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Point your assistant to any 3D STEP file on disk. It reads every tube body, measures wall thickness and OD, and tags each cut feature:
            </p>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> Example: analyze_step Tool Call</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>// Assistant calls analyze_step:
{
  "path": "/Users/operator/Desktop/rollcage-main-hoop.step"
}

// Tool output:
{
  "process_id": "1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA",
  "total_solids": 2,
  "solids": [
    {
      "solid_id": "solid_1",
      "profile": "Profile_HSS_Round",
      "dimensions": { "od": 44.45, "inner_radius": 19.125, "thickness": 3.1, "length": 1820.0 },
      "features": [
        { "id": "feat_01", "type": "startCut", "is_cope_or_fishmouth": false },
        { "id": "feat_02", "type": "endCut", "is_cope_or_fishmouth": true, "x_center": 1812.5 }
      ]
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Any angled saddle joint or tube cope is flagged with <code class="mono">is_cope_or_fishmouth: true</code> so you know right away where zero-bevel prep applies.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-7">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Apply Zero-Bevel Saddle Compensation (<code class="mono">apply_zero_bevel</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              When your perpendicular plasma torch cuts an angled fishmouth on round pipe, the back wall tilts inward and leaves a weld gap. Run zero-bevel compensation to shift your cut path to the inner-wall curve for tight TIG or MIG fit-up:
            </p>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> Example: apply_zero_bevel Tool Call</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>// Assistant calls apply_zero_bevel:
{
  "process_id": "1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA",
  "intensity": 60.0,
  "fade_angle": 90.0,
  "side_gap": 0.5
}

// Tool output:
{
  "process_id": "1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA",
  "intensity": 60.0,
  "applied_count": 1,
  "skipped_count": 1,
  "features": [
    {
      "feature_id": "feat_02",
      "feature_type": "endCut",
      "action": "applied",
      "reason": "Round stock cope/fishmouth cut compensated at 60.0% intensity"
    },
    {
      "feature_id": "feat_01",
      "feature_type": "startCut",
      "action": "skipped",
      "reason": "Straight 90 deg cut (not a cope or saddle fishmouth)"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Straight 90° cuts are skipped automatically, so compensation only shifts true cope and saddle joints.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 8
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-8">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 08</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Generate G-Code &amp; Inspect in 3D (<code class="mono">generate_gcode</code> &amp; <code class="mono">open_in_rotarycam</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Once your cuts and bevel settings look right, write your <code class="mono">.nc</code> stick program to disk and pop open the 3D preview:
            </p>

            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> Generating G-Code &amp; Browser Handoff</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>// 1. Assistant calls generate_gcode:
{
  "process_id": "1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA",
  "mode": "auto"
}

// Output:
{
  "status": "success",
  "files_written": [
    {
      "path": "/Users/operator/Desktop/pack_Profile_HSS_Round_OD44.5_stick1.nc",
      "line_count": 1562,
      "pieces_in_group": 2,
      "total_sticks": 1
    }
  ]
}

// 2. Assistant calls open_in_rotarycam:
{
  "process_id": "1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA"
}

// Tool output:
"Opened session 1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA in RotaryCAM viewport at: http://localhost:3000/process/1B8C4779-9924-43AD-B636-7E1B4B829DEA"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
              Your browser opens the 3D viewport right away so you can check your kerf offset, pierce points, and 4-axis motion path. Before you strike an arc, do a quick dry run to confirm chuck clearance, torch retract height, and IHS/THC settings on your table.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
